<p>Port Moody is a small city that asks a lot of a new driver. In the space of ten minutes you can go from a quiet residential street in Moody Centre to a steep climb up Heritage Mountain, then onto a highway merge where traffic is already doing eighty. If you have booked an ICBC road test and you live in Port Moody, that variety is the thing to prepare for. This guide covers what the terrain demands, where you can practise legally and safely, and the habits that decide Port Moody road tests.</p>

<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Port Moody has no test centre of its own. Your road test runs out of the <strong>Coquitlam ICBC Driver Licensing office</strong>, which serves the whole Tri-Cities.</li>
<li>The local terrain is unusually mixed: steep residential hills, a busy arterial in St Johns Street, and a genuine highway merge onto the Barnet.</li>
<li><strong>Hill starts and parking on a grade</strong> are the skills most worth over-practising here.</li>
<li>Low sun over the inlet is a real hazard on winter afternoons. Clean glass and a working visor matter more than people expect.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where Port Moody road tests start</h2>
<p>Because testing for the Tri-Cities is centralised, your appointment will almost certainly begin at the Coquitlam licensing office rather than anywhere in Port Moody itself. That has a practical consequence people miss: the roads immediately around the test centre are not the roads outside your front door. If every practice hour you have logged is on Port Moody's quiet streets, the first few minutes of the drive will feel unfamiliar.</p>
<p>Give yourself at least a couple of sessions in the Coquitlam area before the day. Not to memorise anything, which is neither possible nor useful, but so the road width, the signal timing and the traffic density feel normal rather than new.</p>

<h2>The hills are the local signature</h2>
<p>Heritage Mountain and the streets climbing away from the inlet are what make driving here different from Port Coquitlam or Burnaby. Anyone who learned entirely on flat ground tends to discover three weaknesses at once.</p>
<h3>Rolling back on a hill start</h3>
<p>The fix is unglamorous: find a moderate grade, stop, and practise moving away without letting the car creep backwards, until it stops being a thing you think about. A small roll is not automatically a fail, but it signals a lack of control, and it tends to arrive with other nerves attached.</p>
<h3>Parking on a grade</h3>
<p>Kerbing the wheels correctly is one of the few things on a road test with a single right answer, which makes it free marks. Downhill, wheels turned toward the kerb. Uphill with a kerb, turned away. Uphill with no kerb, turned toward the shoulder. Say it out loud while you do it and it sticks.</p>
<h3>Speed control coming down</h3>
<p>Long descents tempt you into creeping over the limit without noticing. Examiners notice. Practise using a lower gear rather than riding the brake all the way down.</p>

<h2>St Johns Street: the corridor that catches people out</h2>
<p>St Johns is Port Moody's spine, and it packs a lot into a short distance: driveways, bus stops, pedestrian crossings, parked cars and traffic that changes speed constantly. Lane changes here need proper shoulder checks and a gap you have actually judged rather than hoped for.</p>
<p>The habit worth building is scanning further ahead than feels necessary. Most of the sudden braking on St Johns is avoidable if you saw the brake lights three cars up.</p>

<h2>Merging onto the Barnet</h2>
<p>The Barnet Highway is where nervous drivers give themselves away. Merging too slowly is the classic error: you arrive at the end of the ramp doing well under the speed of traffic, and now you need a gap twice as large as the one you would have needed at speed.</p>
<p>Use the ramp for what it is. Build speed, pick your gap early, shoulder check, and move decisively. If highway merging is the part that worries you most, our <a href="/ultimate-highway-merging-checklist/">highway merging checklist</a> breaks it down step by step.</p>

<h2>Weather and light</h2>
<p>Two local conditions are worth planning for. Rain sits on the road surface here for a long stretch of the year, so following distances need to grow and braking needs to start earlier. And on clear winter afternoons the sun sits low over Burrard Inlet, straight into your eyes on some westbound stretches. A clean windscreen and a working sun visor genuinely change what you can see.</p>

<h2>Where to practise legally</h2>
<p>Stick to public roads and ordinary conditions. Quiet residential streets around Moody Centre and Glenayre are good for early skills. Once those feel automatic, move to the harder stuff at sensible times of day: the hill network above Newport Village for grades, and St Johns outside rush hour for traffic.</p>
<p>Practising at 7pm on a Sunday is comfortable but it does not prepare you for a Tuesday morning test. Somewhere in your preparation you need drives at the time of day your appointment is booked for.</p>

<h2>Common questions</h2>
<h3>Where do Port Moody road tests actually start?</h3>
<p>At the Coquitlam ICBC Driver Licensing office, which handles testing for Port Moody, Coquitlam and Port Coquitlam. Check your booking confirmation for the address and arrival time.</p>
<h3>Do I need to practise on hills specifically?</h3>
<p>If you are testing anywhere in the Tri-Cities, yes. Hill starts and parking on a grade come up often enough that going in without practice is a gamble you do not need to take.</p>
<h3>Can I use a BuckleUp car for the test?</h3>
<p>Yes. Our dual-control vehicles are insured for road tests, and taking the test in a car you have already driven removes one variable on a day that has plenty.</p>
<h3>How many lessons should I take before the test?</h3>
<p>It depends entirely on where you are starting. Some drivers need a single warm-up session, others want several weeks. An honest assessment lesson will tell you more than any average.</p>

<p>Ready to prepare properly? Our <a href="/services/class-5-driving-lessons/">ICBC road test preparation</a> covers exactly what examiners score, and you can see how we run <a href="/locations/port-moody/">driving lessons in Port Moody</a> if you would rather start closer to home. Testing elsewhere in the region? We have guides to <a href="/icbc-road-test-routes-coquitlam-tri-cities/">road tests in Coquitlam and the Tri-Cities</a> and <a href="/icbc-road-test-routes-port-coquitlam/">Port Coquitlam</a> too.</p>
