<p>Port Coquitlam has one feature almost no other city in Metro Vancouver shares in the same concentration: level railway crossings, scattered right through the places you drive every day. Add the Mary Hill Bypass, the Lougheed Highway corridor and the climb up toward Citadel Heights, and a PoCo road test asks for a specific mix of skills. Here is what the terrain demands and how to prepare for it.</p>

<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Port Coquitlam tests run out of the <strong>Coquitlam ICBC Driver Licensing office</strong>, which covers the Tri-Cities.</li>
<li><strong>Rail crossings</strong> are the local signature. Knowing exactly what to do at one is worth practising until it is automatic.</li>
<li>The <strong>Mary Hill Bypass and Lougheed Highway</strong> mean real merging and lane discipline at speed.</li>
<li>Downtown PoCo adds tight parking, frequent pedestrians and a lot of stop-and-go.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Rail crossings: the thing PoCo drivers must get right</h2>
<p>Most learners have crossed tracks a few times. Fewer have thought about what good technique looks like, and it shows under test conditions.</p>
<p>The basics are simple and worth rehearsing. Slow on approach rather than arriving at full speed. Look both ways properly, moving your head so it is visible that you looked. Never stop on the tracks, which means never entering a crossing unless the road beyond it is clear enough to drive off onto. If lights are flashing or the barrier is moving, you stop, and you wait until it is fully up and the lights have stopped.</p>
<p>The mistake that costs marks is subtler than any of that: drifting over a crossing while still watching the car in front, without ever checking the line itself. Build the head movement in deliberately and it becomes habit.</p>

<h2>The Mary Hill Bypass and Lougheed Highway</h2>
<p>Both roads carry fast, heavy traffic including a lot of trucks, and both punish hesitation. Merging is the skill that matters. Get up to the speed of the lane you are joining before you try to join it, choose your gap while you still have ramp left, shoulder check, and commit.</p>
<p>Lane discipline matters just as much. Sitting in the left lane when you are not passing, or changing lanes twice in quick succession to get around someone, both read as poor planning. Look far enough ahead that your lane choice is made early.</p>
<p>Trucks deserve specific respect here. Leave more space than feels necessary, and never sit where a driver cannot see you.</p>

<h2>Downtown PoCo and Shaughnessy Street</h2>
<p>The downtown core is where the test gets fiddly rather than fast. Expect angle parking, cars reversing out of spaces, cyclists, and pedestrians who step out mid-block. Speeds are low, but the observation demand is high.</p>
<p>This is the part of town where a habit of checking mirrors every few seconds pays off. It is also where parking gets tested in a genuinely tight space rather than an empty lot, so practise in real spaces with real cars either side.</p>

<h2>The climb toward Citadel Heights</h2>
<p>PoCo is flatter than Port Moody, but it is not flat. The residential streets rising toward Citadel Heights give you the grades you need to practise hill starts and kerbing your wheels when parking. If your test is in the Tri-Cities, do not skip this because your own neighbourhood happens to be level.</p>

<h2>Where to practise</h2>
<p>Quiet residential areas around Birchland Manor and Riverwood are good for building the basics without pressure. Once those are solid, move deliberately toward the harder ground: a few crossings, a Lougheed merge, and a session downtown when the shops are open and the streets are busy.</p>
<p>Practise in the weather too. Rain changes braking distances and it changes how well you can see a cyclist in your blind spot.</p>

<h2>Common questions</h2>
<h3>Where is the test centre for Port Coquitlam?</h3>
<p>Testing is handled at the Coquitlam ICBC Driver Licensing office, which serves Port Coquitlam, Coquitlam and Port Moody. Your booking confirmation has the address and arrival details.</p>
<h3>What should I do at a railway crossing on a road test?</h3>
<p>Slow down on approach, look along the tracks both ways with a clear head movement, and only cross when the far side is clear enough to drive off onto. Stop for flashing lights or a moving barrier and wait until it is fully up.</p>
<h3>Is the Mary Hill Bypass part of every test?</h3>
<p>No one can tell you what any individual route will include, and you should be wary of anyone who claims to. Prepare so that a fast merge would not surprise you, and the question stops mattering.</p>
<h3>Can I take the test in your car?</h3>
<p>Yes. Our cars are dual-control and insured for road tests, and sitting the test in a familiar vehicle takes one unknown off the day.</p>

